    "type": "question",
    "label": "70",
    "prompt": "Solve the following equation.\n$$\\sqrt{x+5}+\\sqrt{x-2}=7$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=11$.\n\nSet $u=\\sqrt{x+5}$ and $v=\\sqrt{x-2}$; use $u+v=7$ and $u^2-v^2=7$.",
      "worked": "The domain is $x\\geq2$. Let $u=\\sqrt{x+5}$ and $v=\\sqrt{x-2}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}u+v&=7\\\\u^2-v^2&=(x+5)-(x-2)\\\\u^2-v^2&=7\\\\(u-v)(u+v)&=7\\\\u-v&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nAdding the sum and difference gives $u=4$, so $v=3$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x+5&=16\\\\x&=11.\\end{align*}$$\nCheck: $\\sqrt{16}+\\sqrt9=4+3=7$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
